div.div:
int n = 10000;
int size = n * sizeof(float); —> 40,000 bytes
Why: the GPU driver and malloc() only understand raw byte counts, not "elements" — so every allocation converts element-count × sizeof(type) into bytes up front.

Allocate HOST memory:
A_h = (float*)malloc(size);
B_h = (float*)malloc(size);
C_h = (float*)malloc(size);
Why: these live in ordinary CPU RAM (the process heap). malloc() only knows about bytes, so the (float*) cast tells the compiler how to interpret and index that raw block as an array of floats.

for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
A_h[i] = 1.0f;
B_h[i] = 2.0f;
}
Why: C_h is deliberately left uninitialized (garbage) here — it exists only to receive the GPU's answer later. If verification ran before vecAdd(), C_h would contain junk.

int correct = 1;
for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
if (C_h[i] != 3.0f) {
printf("Error at index %d..."); correct=0; break;
}
}
Why 3.0f exactly (no epsilon/tolerance needed): 1.0f + 2.0f is exactly representable in IEEE-754 float — no rounding error, so a strict != comparison is safe here.

free(A_h);
free(B_h);
free(C_h);
Why: releases the CPU heap memory back to the OS. Note there is nothing to free on the device here — vecAdd() already cudaFree'd A_d/B_d/C_d before it returned.

Part 1 — Setup
float *A_d, *B_d, *C_d;
Why: these are just pointer VARIABLES sitting in CPU stack memory. Right now they are uninitialized — they will hold GPU (device) addresses only after cudaMalloc runs. The _d suffix (device) vs _h (host) is a naming convention, not a language rule — it's how the programmer tracks which memory space each pointer is valid in.

cudaMalloc((void**)&A_d, size);
cudaMalloc((void**)&B_d, size);
cudaMalloc((void**)&C_d, size);
Why (void**): cudaMalloc must WRITE the new GPU address into your pointer variable. To modify a variable through a function call, C needs the address OF that variable — i.e. a pointer to the pointer. It's typed void** because cudaMalloc is type-agnostic; the cast tells the compiler to accept any pointer type there. Each call reserves size bytes in DEVICE GLOBAL MEMORY (VRAM) — completely separate physical RAM from the host.

cudaMemcpy(A_d, A_h, size, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);
cudaMemcpy(B_d, B_h, size, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);
Why: host (CPU) and device (GPU) have physically separate DRAM. The GPU cannot reach into main()'s heap on its own — every byte has to be explicitly shipped across the PCIe bus. C_h/C_d are NOT copied here: there is no output yet, and copying it would be wasted bandwidth.
(see the Memory Map diagram below)

Part 2 — Kernel Launch Configuration
int threadsPerBlock = 256;
int blocksPerGrid = (n + threadsPerBlock - 1) / threadsPerBlock;
= (10000 + 255) / 256 = 40
Why the "+255": plain integer division n/256 TRUNCATES (10000/256 = 39.06 → 39), which would drop the last 16 elements entirely. Adding (threadsPerBlock-1) before dividing forces a round-UP (ceiling) division, guaranteeing every element gets a thread even when n is not an exact multiple of 256.

vecAddKernel<<<blocksPerGrid, threadsPerBlock>>>(A_d, B_d, C_d, n);
(async — this line returns to the CPU immediately)
Why: <<<grid, block>>> is CUDA's launch syntax — here it means "create 40 blocks of 256 threads" = 10,240 threads, all running the same kernel code. Launching is asynchronous: the CPU queues the work and moves on to the next C++ statement without waiting.

cudaMemcpy(C_h, C_d, size, cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost);
Why this line BLOCKS: on the default stream, cudaMemcpy is implicitly synchronizing — the CPU stalls here until every one of the 10,240 threads launched above has finished, THEN the driver copies the finished C_d buffer back across PCIe into C_h.
(see the Memory Map diagram below)

Part 3 — Cleanup
cudaFree(A_d);
cudaFree(B_d);
cudaFree(C_d);
Why: returns the three device allocations to the GPU's memory pool. Skipping this would leak VRAM for the lifetime of the process (or until the CUDA context is destroyed).
